body:
Found 2026-08-02 during an end-to-end focus-area test on the demo account (coach `njrini99@`, player Cole Bennett).

## A brand-new focus area reads "92% there" before the player has swung a club

Prescribed a focus area for Cole Bennett through the coach modal: **Fairways Hit %**, his real current value **61%**, suggested target **66%**. Before he had played a single round under it, both the coach board and his own card rendered:

> Fairways Hit % — Now **61** → Target **66** — **92% there**

with the progress bar 92% full, and `aria-label="Fairways Hit %: 92% toward target"`.

The modal says it out loud too, at the moment of creation: *"Starting at 92% of target."*

## Cause

`getProgressPercent` (`src/components/fairway/pages/coachhelm/areaTypes.ts:367`) is a **ratio of absolute values**, not progress along the journey:

```ts
export function getProgressPercent(current, target, targetMetric): number {
  if (current == null || target == null || target === 0) return 0;
  if (isLowerIsBetter(targetMetric)) {
    if (current <= target) return 100;
    return Math.round((target / current) * 100);
  }
  if (target < 0) return 0;
  return Math.min(100, Math.round((current / target) * 100));
}
```

Consumed by `FocusAreaCard.tsx:393` → rendered at `:435` (`{pct}% there`) and `:451` (the bar's a11y label).

For any metric with a non-zero floor this is close to meaningless. The full 61 → 66 journey — the entire objective, a 5-point gain in driving accuracy — moves the bar from **92% to 100%**. Eight percentage points of bar travel represent 100% of the work. Real improvement is invisible; the bar looks nearly done on day zero.

## Goals already do this correctly

`FairwayGoalCard.tsx:160-190` computes progress the right way:

```ts
if (g.baseline_value === null || g.target_value === null || g.current_value === null) { ... }
const span     = g.target_value - g.baseline_value;
const traveled = g.current_value - g.baseline_value;
```

…and has an explicit honest state for the not-yet-started case — *"Not started — baseline captured"* — precisely so a 0% bar doesn't read as failure. Focus areas have no equivalent.

## Why focus areas can't do it today

`golf_player_focus_areas` **has no baseline column**. Full column list: `id, player_id, team_id, coach_id, area_type, title, description, status, target_metric, current_value, target_value, started_at, completed_at, notes, created_at, updated_at, from_review_id, from_insight_id, review_context, progress_notes, priority, outcome_status, target_kind, target_date, target_rounds`.

The value captured at creation *is* `current_value` — and the auto-tracker overwrites it in place (`progress-drivers.ts`, `evaluateAreas`: `.update({ current_value: next, ... })`). So the starting point is destroyed on the first update and a baseline-relative bar cannot even be reconstructed after the fact. Confirmed on the test area: created at `current_value = 61`, and after the cron ran it was `82` with no record that it ever started at 61.

## Proposed fix

1. Add `baseline_value numeric` to `golf_player_focus_areas`; write it at creation (and at acceptance, when `started_at` is stamped — that is the honest window start) and never let the tracker touch it.
2. Backfill existing rows from `current_value` where the area has not yet been tracked (`updated_at = created_at`); leave the rest null.
3. Switch `getProgressPercent` to `(current - baseline) / (target - baseline)`, direction-aware, and add the same "Not started — baseline captured" state goals have. Where baseline is null, render the `Now → Target` pair with **no** percentage rather than falling back to the ratio.

Related: #1242 (the same function inverts outright for unrecognised lower-is-better metrics), #1241 (focus areas also keep no snapshot history, so there is no trajectory to show either).

Found 2026-08-02 testing announcement targeting end to end on the live demo team (Demo University Golf).

## What happens

An announcement targeted at specific players is filtered out for non-recipients **in application code only**. The RLS policy on `golf_announcements` is team-scoped, so any team member can read every announcement for their team — including ones deliberately targeted away from them — by querying PostgREST directly with their own token.

## Evidence

Created "QA TARGETED ANNOUNCEMENT" targeted at Dylan Brooks + Mason Rivers only. Then, by role impersonation in a rolled-back transaction:

```sql
begin;
set local role authenticated;
select set_config('request.jwt.claims','{"sub":"<cole_user_id>","role":"authenticated"}',true);
select count(*) all_ann, count(*) filter (where title like 'QA TARGETED%') sees_qa
from golf_announcements;
rollback;
```

| identity | all announcements | sees the targeted one |
|---|---|---|
| Cole Bennett — **not** targeted | 5 | **1** |
| Dylan Brooks — targeted | 5 | 1 |
| Coach (control) | 5 | 1 |

The coach control returning 5 proves the probe is not vacuous. Cole is a plain team player who was explicitly excluded, and RLS still hands him the row.

The UI is correct — `getAnnouncementsWithMetaImpl` filters properly (`src/app/golf/actions/announcements.ts:576-583`):

```ts
if (!isCoach && playerId) {
  const filtered = enriched.filter(ann => {
    const recipients = recipientsByAnn[ann.id] || [];
    if (recipients.length === 0) return true; // all team
    return recipients.some(r => r.player_id === playerId);
  });
```

Confirmed in the browser: signed in as Cole, he sees his own targeted announcement and **not** the Dylan/Mason one (5 total vs the coach's 6). So there is no leak through the app — the gap is that targeting has no enforcement below it.

## Why it still matters

- The feature's stated contract is "send to specific players." Anyone on the roster can bypass it with the anon key plus their own session — no privilege escalation needed.
- `announcements.ts:464` already reaches for `createAdminClient()` to read `golf_announcement_recipients`, precisely *because* the recipients table's own RLS is tight (`golf_ann_recipients_select_own`). The recipient list is protected; the announcement body it gates is not.
- Blast radius is same-team only, which is why this is MEDIUM rather than HIGH.

## Proposed fix

Add a recipient-aware `SELECT` policy on `golf_announcements` for the player role — visible when the caller is a team member **and** (no rows exist in `golf_announcement_recipients` for that announcement **or** a row exists for the calling player). Coaches keep the team-wide policy. That mirrors the app filter exactly, so the UI needs no change and the app-layer filter becomes defense-in-depth instead of the only defense.

Worth doing alongside #1222, which is already open against `golf_announcement_recipients` policies.

## Problem
`EventsClient` initializes `loading` to `true` but only calls `fetchEvents()` when `teams.length > 0`. A showcase coach with no teams never clears loading.

## Evidence
- `src/app/baseball/(dashboard)/dashboard/events/EventsClient.tsx` line 107: `useState(true)`
```172:175:src/app/baseball/(dashboard)/dashboard/events/EventsClient.tsx
    if (teams.length > 0) {
      fetchEvents();
    }
  }, [authLoading, coach?.id, teams]);
```
When `teams` is `[]` and auth is ready, `fetchEvents` never runs and `loading` stays `true`.

## Why it matters
The Events page is stuck on a skeleton forever for new showcase orgs — a broken empty state.

## Acceptance criteria
- When auth is ready and `teams.length === 0`, set `loading` false and show the empty state.
- No perpetual "Loading…" subtitle/header.
